    {"id": 6, "type": "manual",
     "desc": "Open response with a labelled sketch: two dilations use the same scale factor 2 but different centres. Describe how the two images differ, then explain how to recover the centre of a dilation when one point and its image are known. Read by hand. A full-credit answer says that the two images are congruent to each other but sit in different places, because a dilation moves every point along the ray from ITS OWN centre; and that the centre can be recovered by drawing the line through each point and its image and taking the intersection of two such lines, since the centre is the one point the dilation leaves fixed.",
